attention ahead of that summit  well they will and i think we've been  prepared for it i mean obviously there  was some hint that the the report was  going to suggest pretty bad news in  terms of what has been achieved so far  and what the various pledges will be  achieving  and of course it's a wake-up call and  boris johnson himself has been telling  everyone not to expect perhaps too much  because  that gap is enormous in the sense of  what needs to be done extra if we are to  get even near  achieving uh the 1.5 it looks like on  current projections if we believe the  the figures are coming out now that  we're going to be way above that  and what needs to happen  is that efforts should be concentrated  very much on what goes on between now  and 2030 and what i find quite  surprising actually  is uh that despite the fact that both  the uk of course and the eu seem to have  quite ambitious plans that they have put  together which will be reducing  uh carbon emissions very significantly  from 1990 levels um with the eu of  course pledging 55 percent the the uk  pledging even higher figures by 2030 to  2035 um obviously the big emitters the  us china and of course up to a point  also india don't look like they're doing  enough i'm guessing that is why it looks  so bad in terms of what the pledges are  telling us  simon the warning came that if there's  no meaningful change we will have lost  forever the chance of achieving 1.5  celsius and only 2.7 degree rises in  temperatures this century in practical  terms what would that mean what would it  look like  it's a world we really don't want to  find out about uh  large areas of the earth would have  unlivable heat  at uh parts of the year  we would see the kind of climate impacts  we saw last summer of massive heat waves  in canada  of floods washing houses into  the  rivers in germany and people up to their  necks in water on the subway system in  china happening with great frequency and  for me i think it's very hard to see how  agriculture globally can adapt to that  kind of level  of change uh and keep food supplies  going for what will at that point be uh  eight or nine billion people on earth it  would be a real catastrophe and world  leaders um  in in these next two weeks really need  to um  push hard to have practical policies to  reduce emissions in the short term  and simon we heard that warning from the  prime minister yesterday that that he's  really worried  we've had saudi arabia's pledges we've  had australia today but the problem has  been highlighted that many of those net  zero goals remain vague we haven't got  the details of them and much of the  heavy lifting is pushed to after  2030 is anything in glasgow going to  change that  well i do think that these net zero  pledges are important because they bring  climate action  two decades earlier and that means that  we then start talking about what is  going to happen in the 2020s and what to  watch out for at this summit is the  first two days when more than 120 world  leaders will be in glasgow making  pledges and making announcements and  that's the time to look for potentially  big announcements about what might  happen over the next decade  vicki and simon stay with us because  another report released today shed new  light on our online data habits in  particular storing photos it's claimed  that unwanted digital photos produce the  same amount of carbon emissions as more  than a hundred thousand return flights  from london to australia so do we all  need to take more responsibility for  tackling the climate crisis  vicky  we think about what we do at home don't  we we think about the way in which we  use energy we think about the cars that  we drive we think about the things that  we eat for example i've never even  considered  my phone and the photographs that i take  as being a factor here  well absolutely and i have to say when i  read the report i immediately looked at  all my photographs well i didn't look at  them all because of course there are so  many  i like everyone else is keeping rather a  lot of data on our phones and computers  that perhaps we don't need to do um but  the knowledge that um  platforms like  whether it's facebook google  um for example  are in fact making us all and they  themselves consume an awful lot of  energy it's been around for some time  and  the figures are such that suggest that  in fact the energy consumed through all  our uses if you like is something  equivalent to  airline emissions  global airline emissions at present  which is pretty large that's about three  to four percent  of global emissions and of course there  is a huge increase in the use and  although we've read about this of course  we individually didn't think that just  googling something uh a few times also  of course you know emits  at the end of the day a lot of co2 um  what of our worry about is that this  report says what we as individuals  should do but obviously policymakers  should know that this is happening the  data has been out there for some time  and we know that it's going to be even  bigger in the future i mean the question  now with post covet and us all using a  lot of social media and other ways in  terms of communicating that is supposed  to be good for the environment i think  something needs to give um so we can't  just be living in this ignorance or at  least most people not realizing that  what they're doing is also contributing  to worsening the environment and  i don't know and i haven't quite thought  about what the implications of this can  be whether indeed uh you know all those  platforms can be more energy efficient  but obviously they need to okay and  simon i did the same as vicky i looked  immediately when i read this at the  number of photos on my phone i don't  even want to admit it  um  vicky talked about the responsibility of  individuals is there something we can  all do to clean up our habits and what  about the companies themselves  well i looked at this report as a  conscientious scientist and looked at  the calculations behind it and what  they're saying is that all of this  social media and internet activity is  about 0.2 percent  of our emissions over the whole year so  it's very very small and we need to keep  in mind the big picture here if we get  our electricity system down to zero  emissions through renewables then we can  then take as many photographs as we like  vicki simon thanks to both of you  well that is everything from us for  today joining us on tomorrow's show is  greenpeace uk's head of climate kate  blagojevich and organic farmer and  deputy president of the national farmers  union stuart roberts  thanks for watching we'll see you next  time
 

